# The memory bank is a record: append a file, never edit a shared one. That rule is
# what keeps it out of every pull request's conflict list, and these two lines are the
# safety net for the one file the rule cannot cover.
#
# `merge=union` takes BOTH sides of a conflicting hunk instead of stopping. It is right
# here and wrong almost everywhere else: it is only safe for a file whose lines are
# unordered, independent, and meaningless in combination with their neighbours. An index
# of one-line-per-record is exactly that. A config file, a lockfile or anything with
# syntax is not - union would happily produce a file that parses as neither side.
#
# The cost is that a genuine edit to the SAME line on both sides silently keeps both
# copies rather than asking. For an index that means one duplicated row, visible in the
# next `rk memory find` and fixed by deleting a line. That is the trade, taken knowingly.
memory-bank/index.md      merge=union
memory-bank/decisions/*   merge=union
